  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 324 AM EDT Sun Oct 25 2026\n\nWhile not much of a legend down here as it is up in the Great Lakes, \nit appears that the gales of November may indeed come early for \nSouth Florida. Breezy 25 to 35 mph wind gusts will continue to be \ncommon Monday night through Tuesday morning. Strong surface high \npressure over the mid-Atlantic states will continue to induce a \ntight pressure gradient along the Florida Atlantic coast through the \nend of the week. As a result, breezy conditions will continue at \nleast through Wednesday. As discussed in the short term \ndiscussion, whether a wind advisory will also be needed for the \nMonday night and Tuesday period is still up for debate. It seems \nthat the most likely areas to see sustained winds of 30 mph or \ngreater is along the immediate coasts of Broward, Dade, and Palm \nBeach counties. Probabilities for advisory criteria winds are only\nat about 40%, so most areas will likely only see sustained winds \nof around 20 mph. Pressure gradients begin to relax near the \nmiddle of the week, which will finally lead to some calming of the\nwinds. However, surface high pressure along the eastern seaboard \nwill keep the same overall pattern in place, with northeast winds\npersisting into next weekend at around 10 to 15 mph.\n\nEnsemble clusters are showing relatively good agreement regarding \nthe upper level pattern heading into the middle of the week. A \nseries of shortwave perturbations moving over the intermountain west \nwill begin to flatten and steer high pressure over the Gulf and \nDeep South. Meanwhile, a cut off low over the Bahamas will begin \nto eject out into the Atlantic by Wednesday, which should allow \nfor the weather to become a little less unsettled. As the mid-\nlevel ridge begins to exert greater influence over South Florida,\nchances of precipitation will decline and high temperatures look \nto climb back up into the upper 80s. The cut off low near the \nBahamas will help transport some increased moisture into the \nnorthern Caribbean, with PWATs looking to climb to near 1.8 inches\nover South Florida (if the systems stays close enough to our \narea). Overall, this northeasterly flow pattern will favor \nscattered showers and storms coming off of the Gulf Stream waters \nfor most of the week. Lightning and locally heavy rainfall will be\nthe main threats, but there is currently no risk of excessive \nrainfall after Tuesday. A more typical sea breeze induced \nconvective pattern will likely return once northeast winds weaken \ntowards the end of the week.\n\nThe potential for moderate and major tidal flooding may continue \nduring the middle portions of the week. This tidal flooding may also \nbe exacerbated by coastal showers and strong northeast winds, which \ncould lead to significant water rises along the far eastern metro \nareas. While the peak tides will be during the beginning to middle \nof the week, moderate coastal flooding may persist through the end \nof the week as we come out of this full moon phase.",
